  "description": "Learn essential food and drink vocabulary, and how to say what you want, eat, and drink in Chinese.",
  "guidebook": "## Talking About Food in Chinese 🥢\n\nIn this unit, you'll learn the most common food and drink words you'll encounter every day.\n\n**The verb 想 (xiǎng)** means \"to want\" and is super useful when ordering food or expressing cravings. Pair it with a noun: **我想吃苹果** (wǒ xiǎng chī píngguǒ) — \"I want to eat an apple.\"\n\n**Two key verbs to remember:**\n- **吃 (chī)** = \"to eat\" — used for solid food\n- **喝 (hē)** = \"to drink\" — used for liquids like 水, 茶, and 果汁\n\n**Measure words matter!** Chinese uses measure words (量词) when counting nouns. For most food items, use **个 (gè)**: 一个鸡蛋 (yī gè jīdàn) — \"one egg.\"\n\n**Saying \"I want...\":**\n- 我要咖啡 (wǒ yào kāfēi) — \"I want coffee\" (more direct, like ordering)\n- 我想喝水 (wǒ xiǎng hē shuǐ) — \"I want to drink water\" (more polite)\n\n**Tones tip:** 喝 (hē, 1st tone) and 吃 (chī, 1st tone) are both high and flat — easy to remember together!",
